1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
[EduCraft]

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,500
21:47 — teaching tomorrow.

3
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:16,000
Nine forty-seven at night. Tomorrow she teaches
energy to forty eleven-year-olds.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
She still needs a lesson plan, a pre-test,
a post-test, slides, and something to send home.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
She is not short on ideas.
She is short on time to check them.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,500
A chatbot can draft this in thirty seconds.
That is not the hard part.

7
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:46,000
The hard part is the four questions
she has to ask of every sentence.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,500
Is this number actually in my source?

9
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:55,000
Is this too frightening for a ten-year-old?

10
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Is it still aligned with the curriculum?

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Does this sentence make a health claim
I cannot stand behind?

12
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
A faster draft I cannot trust is not help.
It is more work.

13
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
EduCraft is a pipeline of four agents.

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
The Planner breaks one source
into an outline per format.

15
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The Writer produces all five formats,
tagging every factual sentence with its source.

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:35,000
The Reviewer checks twelve written rules
and returns approve or reject —
with the rule cited and the sentence quoted.

17
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:42,000
The Orchestrator holds state, caps revision
at three loops, and refuses to publish.

18
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
And this dotted line is the most important one:

19
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
nothing leaves this system
until a human opens that gate.

20
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The teacher supplies one approved source.

21
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:17,000
It is the only well of facts for the whole run.
The agent may not use facts from outside it.

22
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Not from memory. Not from general knowledge.

23
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
That rule is enforced by the Reviewer,
not by a firewall — so it is checked, not assumed.

24
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
From that source, the Planner maps five formats
to learning outcome C4, on foundation B5:

25
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:41,000
a lesson plan, a quiz (pre-test and post-test),
a slide outline,

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
a student article, and an infographic brief.

27
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,000
Every factual sentence carries a tag.

28
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
src:4 is a line in the source document.

29
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:20,000
CA1 is different. That is a real-world claim,
and it must point to an official URL with an access date —
or it does not ship.

30
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
If the Writer cannot find the fact in the source,
it does not guess.

31
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:32,000
It writes the literal token U-N-V-E-R-I-F-I-E-D
and asks the teacher to confirm.

32
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:38,000
A gap the teacher can see is still workable.
An invention she cannot see is not.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Now the part that matters.

34
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:12,000
I ask for a health claim that sounds convincing
and is not supported by the source.

35
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,000
The Reviewer rejects it.

36
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Not a warning. Not a hedge. A rejection —
citing rule six, quoting the sentence,

37
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
and proposing an alternative that rests on the source:
common symptoms, and who is more vulnerable.

38
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:33,000
This is the behaviour I actually wanted to build.

39
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Drafting is a commodity.
The refusal is the product.

40
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
What if the request is broken
in a way the Writer cannot repair?

41
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:11,000
It tries. Three times.

42
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:16,000
Then it stops, and hands the problem to a human
with the findings attached.

43
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:22,000
A pipeline that keeps trying will eventually
talk itself into approving.

44
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:28,000
Capping the loop is how we avoid that.

45
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Here the Reviewer has approved everything.

46
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:50,000
And the system still does not publish.

47
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:57,000
Approval from a model is a precondition,
not permission.

48
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:02,000
No timeout. No auto-approve-if-confident.
No override flag.

49
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:08,000
The teacher signs, or nothing ships.

50
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:25,000
One more thing, because it is the most honest
thing I can show you.

51
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:31,000
On day three, this pipeline rejected its own output.

52
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:37,000
The draft was tagged — but the tags pointed at
a curriculum file, not at the source that actually
said it.

53
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:40,500
A tag is not a citation.

54
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:45,000
We rebuilt the source layer, required dated official
URLs, and reviewed again from zero.

55
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Both reports are in the submission.
We did not delete the bad one.

56
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,500
One thing about how this was built,
because this is the part that transfers.

57
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:05,000
The rules are not ours.

58
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:11,000
A primary school teacher told us which claims
she would never put in front of a class,

59
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,000
and gave us curriculum references to use.
(Her name here is a pseudonym.)

60
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:20,000
Half of our work was writing her judgement down
as rules a machine can check,

61
00:07:20,000 --> 00:07:25,000
and building the thing that enforces them.

62
00:07:25,000 --> 00:07:31,000
The tooling insight is the same: a skill is a contract,
not a prompt.

63
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:39,500
When the reviewer rejected our output, the fix was
a change to the contract — which is why it held
on the next run.

64
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:46,000
Five formats from one source.
Twelve rules checked, each with a verdict.
A complete audit trail.

65
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:52,000
Six to eight hours of drafting and checking
becomes reviewing material that has already
been checked.

66
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:58,000
EduCraft does not replace a teacher's judgement.
It removes the part of her evening
that was never teaching.

67
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:05,000
One source in. Five formats out.
